        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Plurals: five patterns",
        "explicacao": "German plurals come in five patterns: -e (der Tisch → die Tische), -(e)n (die Lampe → die Lampen), -er (das Buch → die Bücher), no ending (der Lehrer → die Lehrer) and -s (das Auto → die Autos, common in foreign words). Many words also add an umlaut to the vowel (a → ä, o → ö, u → ü) — just like English man → men, foot → feet, mouse → mice, which are leftovers of the same old Germanic rule. There's no safe rule: learn the plural with the noun. The plural definite article is always «die», for all three genders.",
        "comparacao": "The English reflex is to add -s. In German, -s is the rarest pattern (mostly foreign words: Autos, Hotels). English already has the German patterns in its oldest words: child → children is the German -er (Kind → Kinder), ox → oxen is -en, man → men is the umlaut (Mann → Männer).",
